{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-98714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node.\n- $y=2$ is a sink.\n- $y=2$ is a source." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b1b6994c254268a7d4d9cdccf817649336d045198929e952bb75c15409474d7c"} {"question" "q-98714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y+4)({y}^{2}-4)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)({y}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y+2)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-2,2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=\\pm 2.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-4,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-2,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/8756839e7b9c77934acec128d098eab5b7482a3b7422945b3507b6379fbc528b.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sources, and $y=-2$ is a sink\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b1b6994c254268a7d4d9cdccf817649336d045198929e952bb75c15409474d7c"} {"question" "q-98759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sinks, and $y=-2$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sources, and $y=-2$ is a node"] ["text" "$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sources, and $y=-2$ is a sink"] ["text" "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=-2$ and $y=2$ are both sources"] ["text" "$y=-4$ is a source, and $y=-2$ and $y=2$ are both sinks"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sources, and $y=-2$ is a sink"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+3y+9$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/244a46b17ad69e8d7cc3ae4f3d9c3c46c1d6e6a7ea154b849946f8ece6030071.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98761" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b1b6994c254268a7d4d9cdccf817649336d045198929e952bb75c15409474d7c"} {"question" "q-98761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y=3$ is a node"] ["text" "$y=3$ is a sink"] ["text" "$y=3$ is a sink and $y=-3$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=3$ is a source"] ["text" "$y=3$ is a source and $y=-3$ is a sink"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98761" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$y=3$ is a source"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=0$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=0$ points downward, the arrow just above it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/db7e9ea076c3a1fe2cf5341b841693e36d5b9a9030e624da88d2cfd3b0d3a1b0.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b1b6994c254268a7d4d9cdccf817649336d045198929e952bb75c15409474d7c"} {"question" "q-98781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/1a9eb4ad4a219ae7b36e418fd51ab15de36940a67b5a8a4aeeef6538e18e551c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/8fd918e5eba15cec6472f84c64f6181920e8d02122919f844678f79aa23e27a0.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/b6bbed5a54701e3e467e62fc1acfff78607c63b9d2fad07e092c826a3bca0c0e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/fa06ae341ff2c761aa3e60de7270bd0ffad885b7e602c12c7af340acc0781818.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-98781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-340357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-340357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/b2cb0682ba91ac23d418d91e7564cbb6245bf498de79b3e615f3ed2b1fcdb997.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-340357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b1b6994c254268a7d4d9cdccf817649336d045198929e952bb75c15409474d7c"} {"question" "q-340357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-340357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/514e6bec32ef0d172c5d3e9df05da8613806bb60faee267702ffa17238d0f826.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/c697a6b80923ffb6c557d59aa2e2924561121c373b4221709e280e5d35297e33.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/c7e7fd7cbf8c3f2f9b68f0af3ecfbdb616730dc7b7cc3b03aa6f35228843080e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/ef9ada6ab016d70b2b14d53cb2f3021793ea78dd69048700c07d5a5161901ecf.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/state.json" "file_sha256" "9cfe629c71b4543e232ba5c580bc97e29b3fa15c544afc6c52179602378483ef"} {"question" "q-340357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/refe